===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[person#Noun|person]] who [[float#Verb|floats]].
## A person who [[physically]] floats in a [[gas#Noun|gas]] or [[liquid#Noun|liquid]].
##* {{RQ:Dryden Metamorphoses|book=IV|section=Alcithoe and Her Sisters Transform'd to Bats|page=126|passage=Great God of VVaters [''i.e.'', [[w:Neptune (mythology)|Neptune]]], vvhoſe extended Svvay / Is next to his, vvhom Heav'n and Earth obey: / Let not the Suit of ''[[w:Venus (mythology)|Venus]]'' thee diſpleaſe, / Pity the '''Floaters''' on th' ''Ionian'' Seas.}}
### {{lb|en|law enforcement|slang}} A [[corpse#Noun|corpse]] floating in a [[body of water]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=E. J. Stauffer|chapter=3|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1eU3DwAAQBAJ&pg=PT19|title=The Drowning|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=[[w:Simon & Schuster|Archway Publishing]]|date=18 September 2017|isbn=978-1-4808-5217-4|passage=A dock worker saw him floating against a dock pier and called the police. Condition of the body they thought a '''floater''' that had been hit by a boat and cut by the prop.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} A person without a [[fixed#Adjective|fixed]] or [[lasting#Adjective|lasting]] [[affiliation]], [[position#Noun|position]], or [[role]].
### A person who [[frequently]] [[change#Verb|changes]] where they [[live#Verb|live]]; a [[drifter]], a [[vagrant#Noun|vagrant]].
###: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:vagabond}}
### A person who frequently changes [[employment]].
### An [[employee]] of an [[organization]] who does not have fixed [[task#Noun|tasks]] to do but [[fill in|fills in]] wherever [[need#Verb|needed]], usually when someone else is away; also, a [[short-term#Adjective|short-term]] employee; a [[temporary#Noun|temporary]], a [[temp#Noun|temp]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Len Deighton|chapter=14|title=w:An Expensive Place to Die|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hamlyn (publisher)|Hamlyn Publishing Group]] for the Companion Book Club|year=1967|page=90|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/expensiveplaceto0000unse/page/90/mode/1up|oclc=13493730|passage='The murdered girl was working for us, for the department.' / 'A '''floater'''?' / 'No. Permanent; warranty contract; the lot.'}}
### An "[[extra#Adjective|extra]]" ([[male#Adjective|male]]) [[guest#Noun|guest]] at a [[party#Noun|party]] who is [[ask#Verb|asked]] by the [[host#Noun|host]] to [[entertain#Verb|entertain]] the other (often [[female#Adjective|female]]) guests.
### {{lb|en|slang}} A person who [[attach]]es themselves to a [[group#Noun|group]] of people, and who [[repeatedly]] [[show up|shows up]] at group [[activity|activities]] even though this is [[undesired]] by the group; a [[hanger-on]].
### {{lb|en|politics}}
#### A [[voter]] who [[shift#Verb|shifts]] their [[allegiance]] from one [[political party]] to another, especially {{lb|en|US}} one whose vote can be [[illegally]] [[purchase#Verb|purchased]].
#### {{lb|en|US}} A person who [[vote#Verb|votes]] illegally in [[various#Determiner|various]] [[electoral district]]s or [[polling place]]s, either under a [[false#Adjective|false]] voter [[registration]] or under the [[name#Noun|name]] of a [[properly]] [[registered#Adjective|registered]] voter who has not yet voted.
#### {{lb|en|US}} A person, such as a [[delegate#Noun|delegate]] to a [[convention]] or a [[member#Noun|member]] of a [[legislature]], who [[represent]]s an [[irregular#Adjective|irregular]] [[constituency]], such as one [[form#Verb|formed]] by a [[union#Noun|union]] of the voters of two [[county|counties]] neither of which has a [[number#Noun|number]] [[sufficient#Determiner|sufficient]] to be [[allow]]ed one (or an extra) [[representative]] of its own.
### {{lb|en|sports}} A [[player]] not [[affiliated#Adjective|affiliated]] with a [[team#Noun|team]].
# A [[thing]] which floats.
## A thing which physically floats in a gas or liquid.
### {{senseid|en|mussel}} A [[river mussel]] ([[genus]] {{taxfmt|Anodonta|genus}}).
### A [[small#Adjective|small]] [[suet]] [[dumpling]] [[put#Verb|put]] into [[soup#Noun|soup]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Cynthia Harrod-Eagles|chapter=9|title=The Fallen Kings|series=[[w:The Morland Dynasty|[The Morland] Dynasty]]|seriesvolume=32|location=London|publisher=[[w:Sphere Books|Sphere]]|year=2009|year_published=2010|page=204|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fallenkings0000harr_p8l2/page/204/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-84744-115-7|passage=About an hour later a ration lorry found them, and the men were woken again, this time to a good meal of bully stew and '''floaters'''{{--}} suet dumplings{{-–}} followed by cold rice pudding with jam.}}
### {{lb|en|vulgar}} A [[piece#Noun|piece]] of [[faeces]] which floats.
###: {{ux|en|He left a '''floater''' in the toilet.}}
### {{senseid|en|ophthalmology}}{{lb|en|ophthalmology}} A [[threadlike]] [[speck#Noun|speck]] in the [[visual field]] which [[seem]]s to move, possibly [[cause#Verb|caused]] by [[degeneration]] of the [[vitreous humour]] of the [[eye#Noun|eye]].
###: {{syn|en|musca volitans}}
### {{senseid|en|sports}}{{lb|en|sports}} A [[ball#Noun|ball]] that [[move#Verb|moves]] [[lightly]] through the [[air#Noun|air]], as if floating; specifically {{lb|en|basketball}}, an [[early#Adjective|early]] [[layup]] [[take#Verb|taken]] by a [[player]] moving towards the [[rim#Noun|rim]] where, upon [[release#Noun|release]], the ball floats in the air over the [[top#Noun|top]] of a [[defender]] before [[drop#Verb|dropping]] [[softly]] into the [[hoop#Noun|hoop]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Tarrence Garrison|title=Basketball Essentials: Learn, Improve, Dominate|location=[Scotts Valley, Calif.]|publisher=&#91;{{w|CreateSpace}}&#93;|year=2014|isbn=978-1-4996-6916-9|passage=The '''floater''' is a high arcing shot that is commonly used to get the ball over taller defenders in the paint.}}
### {{senseid|en|pie}}{{lb|en|Australia}} {{short for|en|pie floater|t=a [[meat pie]] [[serve#Verb|served]] [[float#Verb|floating]] in a [[bowl#Noun|bowl]] of [[thick#Adjective|thick]] [[green pea]] [[soup#Noun|soup]]}}
### {{lb|en|India}} A [[waterproof#Adjective|waterproof]] [[sandal#Noun|sandal]].
###* {{quote-journal|en|author=Mansi Mahendru|title=How to Dress Up during Monsoon: Six Tips|editor=Divesh Nath|journal=[[w:Woman's Era|Woman’s Era]]|location=New Delhi|publisher=Paresh Nath, {{w|Delhi Press}}|date=1 September 2017|volume=44|issue=1050|page=16|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Uwo2DwAAQBAJ&pg=PA16|columns=1–2|issn=0971-1503|oclc=46760617|passage=Replace your leather sandals with rubber slippers, sandals or '''floaters'''. They don't soak water well and why in this world would you want to spoil your expensive leather footwear in puddles?}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} A thing which moves from [[place#Noun|place]] to place, as if floating.
### {{lb|en|slang|dated}} A [[mistake#Noun|mistake]] or [[misstep#Noun|misstep]]; a [[faux pas]].
###: {{synonyms|en|blunder|false move|false step|stumble|Thesaurus:error}}
###* {{RQ:Huxley Those Barren Leaves|chapter=1|page=9|passage=Suddenly, for no reason, in the middle of the night, or even in the middle of the jolliest party, she would remember an ancient '''floater'''—just like that, ''à propos de bottes''—would remember and be overcome by a feeling of self-reproach and retrospective shame. {{...}} One could do one's best; but one could never really persuade oneself that the '''floater''' hadn't happened. Imagination might struggle to annihilate the odious memory; but it never had power to win a decisive victory.}}
###* {{RQ:Waugh Scoop|chapter=2|section=3|page=26|passage=He greeted William with cordiality. "Ah, Boot, how are you? Don't think I've had the pleasure before. Know your work well of course. Sit down. Have a cigarette or"—had he made a '''floater'''?—"or do you prefer your [[churchwarden]]?"}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:P.G. Wodehouse|P[elham] G[renville] Wodehouse]]|chapter=7|title=[[w:The Mating Season (novel)|The Mating Season]]|location=Harmondsworth, Middlesex [London]|publisher=w:Penguin Books|date=9 September 1949|year_published=1957|page=72|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/matingseasonpeng0000pelh/page/72/mode/1up|oclc=18392630|passage=I must let her know I'm here and put her in touch with the general situation so she will be warned against making any '''floaters'''.}}
### {{lb|en|finance}} {{short for|en|[[floating rate]] [[bond#Noun|bond]]}}
### {{lb|en|insurance}} An [[insurance policy]] [[cover#Verb|covering]] [[movable property]] at more than one [[location]] or which may be [[in transit]].
### {{lb|en|prison slang}} A [[book#Noun|book]] [[circulate]]d between [[prisoner]]s which is not [[part#Noun|part]] of the [[official#Adjective|official]] [[prison#Noun|prison]] [[library]].
###* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Michael Tippett|chapter=The Heart’s Assurance|title=Those Twentieth Century Blues: An Autobiography|location=London|publisher=[[w:Hutchinson (publisher)|Hutchinson]]|year=1991|page=143|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/thosetwentiethce0000tipp/page/143/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-09-175307-8|passage=One prisoner came to me and asked if I had anything to read. As I hadn’t, he said he would bring me some reading. The next day he arrived with two serious books under his shirt. ‘'''Floaters'''’ [i.e. unofficially circulated books] had also come into the prison: you could either find them in the library, or they would be given to someone else to keep away from the screws—they were never openly visible.|footer={{small|The words in brackets are in the original text.}}}}
###* {{quote-book|en|author=Julian Broadhead, interviewer|quotee=[[w:Noel "Razor" Smith|Noel “Razor” Smith]]|chapter=The Prison Writing Interview|editors=Julian Broadhead; Laura Kerr|title=Prison Writing 2001: A Collection of Fact, Fiction and Verse|location=Winchester, Hampshire|publisher=Waterside Press|month=November|year=2000|issue=15|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=MRzbCgAAQBAJ&pg=PA9|isbn=978-1-872870-87-8|passage=His &#91;[[w:G. F. Newman|Gordon Frank Newman]]’s&#93; books were ‘'''floaters'''’, banned by the borstal, but copies were smuggled in and from wing to wing. We used to rip the covers off them cos if you got caught with one it was seven days block.}}
### {{lb|en|surfing}} A [[maneuver#Noun|manoeuvre]] in which a [[surfer]] [[transition#Verb|transitions]] above the [[unbroken]] [[face#Noun|face]] of a [[wave#Noun|wave]] on to the [[lip#Noun|lip]], or on top of the [[breaking#Adjective|breaking]] [[section#Noun|section]] of the wave.
### {{lb|en|cricket}} A spinning delivery of the ball that travels in a high arched path.
### {{lb|en|two-up}} A [[coin#Noun|coin]] which does not [[spin#Verb|spin]] when [[throw#Verb|thrown]] in the air.
###* {{quote-book|en|title=Casino Gaming Amendment Rule (No. 2) 1998|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20040730160912/http://www.legislation.qld.gov.au/LEGISLTN/SLS/1998/98SL233.pdf|series=Subordinate Legislation 1998 No. 233|location=[Brisbane, Qld.]|publisher=w:Queensland Government|date=27 July 1998|pages=7–8|passage=[Rule] 477.(1) The boxer for a game of triple penny two-up, or a floor manager, may declare a spin invalid if the spin is a '''floater'''. {{...}} [Rule 477.](6) In this section—"'''floater'''" means a spin in which at least 1 of the coins does not turn over in the air at least once.|footer={{small|This rule amended the Casino Gaming Rules 1985.}}}}
### {{lb|en|US|law}} A [[criminal#Adjective|criminal]] [[sentence#Noun|sentence]] which is [[suspend]]ed so long as the [[convict#Verb|convicted]] person [[leave#Verb|leaves]] an [[area]].
###* {{RQ:Steinbeck East of Eden|chapter=31|section=1|page=383|passage=["]Suppose I should tell you that there's a permanent order in the sheriff's office, left there by the old sheriff, that if I ever use your name or admit I'm your wife I'll get a '''floater''' out of the county and out of the state. Does that tempt you?" / "Tempt me to do what?" / "To get me floated and take all the money."}}

